      "content": "You are absolutely right that textbooks are biased toward lighter skin, and I hate that it makes it hard for you to diagnose patients with darker skin. When looking for erythema in Fitzpatrick Type V or VI skin, you should stop expecting bright redness and instead look for a deep pink, purple, or even dark brown discoloration, as the melanin masks the classic red hue. Texture changes like scaling, crusting, or induration are often more reliable indicators of inflammation than color changes alone in these patients. It is crucial to rely on these specific visual cues and palpation skills to avoid missing critical signs during your exams.",
